WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:01.610
- [Paparazzi] Scarlett,
hold that smile for us.

00:00:01.610 --> 00:00:02.470
- [Narrator] Actors are looking

00:00:02.470 --> 00:00:04.160
to renegotiate their contracts

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:05.370
in big budget movies.

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:06.940
- Scarlett Johansson, the star

00:00:06.940 --> 00:00:09.860
of Disney's latest Marvel
movie, "Black Widow,"

00:00:09.860 --> 00:00:11.960
has filed a lawsuit today

00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:14.340
in Los Angeles Superior
Court against Disney.

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:15.680
- [Narrator] Traditional movie contracts

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:18.390
for A list talent include
sizable upfront payments

00:00:18.390 --> 00:00:20.350
and a lucrative backend deal based mostly

00:00:20.350 --> 00:00:21.880
on box office ticket sales.

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:23.150
But with more films going straight

00:00:23.150 --> 00:00:24.350
to streaming platforms,

00:00:24.350 --> 00:00:26.190
actors are now looking for a piece

00:00:26.190 --> 00:00:27.710
of the streaming profits.

00:00:27.710 --> 00:00:29.460
- The movie wouldn't exist without actors,

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:31.620
and especially actors of certain stature

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:32.600
and with certain names.

00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:34.200
They drive people to those theaters

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:37.970
or they drive people to download
that subscription service,

00:00:37.970 --> 00:00:41.140
and I do believe that they
should get a portion of that.

00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:42.450
- [Narrator] Paying stars a bigger piece

00:00:42.450 --> 00:00:43.830
of digital sales would upend

00:00:43.830 --> 00:00:46.140
the traditional pay structure for talent.

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:48.370
And for some, it could
lead to tens of millions

00:00:48.370 --> 00:00:50.010
of dollars in residual pay.

00:00:50.010 --> 00:00:51.840
Money that agents and actors say

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:54.070
is now being left on the table.

00:00:54.070 --> 00:00:56.160
Here's a look at how the
Scarlett Johansson lawsuit

00:00:56.160 --> 00:00:57.950
against Disney could be a catalyst

00:00:57.950 --> 00:01:00.443
that changes the way
Hollywood stars get paid.

00:01:02.927 --> 00:01:04.190
(paparazzi screaming)

00:01:04.190 --> 00:01:06.280
To understand what's at stake with actors,

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:07.830
you have to understand how their pay

00:01:07.830 --> 00:01:10.120
is tied to a film's distribution cycle.

00:01:10.120 --> 00:01:12.240
- There are so many
unique and creative ways

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:14.290
for actors and performers

00:01:14.290 --> 00:01:16.440
to make additional compensation

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:18.280
for their participation in films.

00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:19.250
- [Narrator] Natasha Matallana,

00:01:19.250 --> 00:01:21.730
who's been representing
actors for over 15 years,

00:01:21.730 --> 00:01:23.440
says depending on the contract,

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:25.010
backend bonuses in the long run

00:01:25.010 --> 00:01:26.500
can be more lucrative for actors

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:27.930
than an upfront payment.

00:01:27.930 --> 00:01:29.893
- Some of that compensation

00:01:29.893 --> 00:01:32.270
is creatively carved out

00:01:32.270 --> 00:01:35.030
by way of calling it a production fee

00:01:35.030 --> 00:01:37.720
or a marketing fee or backend points.

00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:38.760
- [Narrator] Those bonuses are tied

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:39.960
to how well the film does

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:42.100
at each point of the distribution cycle.

00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:44.470
Traditionally, that cycle looks like this.

00:01:44.470 --> 00:01:45.680
Feature films start their journey

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:46.850
with a theatrical release

00:01:46.850 --> 00:01:48.970
that can last anywhere from a few weeks

00:01:48.970 --> 00:01:51.290
to a few months, depending
on how well it does

00:01:51.290 --> 00:01:52.470
in the box office.

00:01:52.470 --> 00:01:54.330
At this stage, talent contracts for actors

00:01:54.330 --> 00:01:57.160
can include a cut of the
opening box office sales.

00:01:57.160 --> 00:01:58.410
After its run in the theaters,

00:01:58.410 --> 00:02:01.620
a film would then make its
way onto DVD or pay per view.

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:02.910
Depending on their contract,

00:02:02.910 --> 00:02:05.130
an actor might make a cut of that as well.

00:02:05.130 --> 00:02:06.360
About six months after that,

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:07.700
the title would become available

00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:10.870
on streaming services,
like Disney+ or Hulu.

00:02:10.870 --> 00:02:13.400
This gives talent another
opportunity to cash in.

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:14.770
This time with licensing deals

00:02:14.770 --> 00:02:17.210
with various streaming content providers.

00:02:17.210 --> 00:02:19.200
Eventually, if a film was well received,

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:21.790
it could also end up
syndicated on television.

00:02:21.790 --> 00:02:23.760
Syndicated films continue to make money

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:24.830
for studios and talent,

00:02:24.830 --> 00:02:27.090
as long as the broadcaster
continues to license

00:02:27.090 --> 00:02:28.210
and air the film.

00:02:28.210 --> 00:02:29.920
So that's what it looked like before.

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:31.780
Here's what it's starting
to look like today.

00:02:31.780 --> 00:02:33.430
Studios like Disney and Warner Media

00:02:33.430 --> 00:02:36.350
are releasing films both in
theaters for cinema goers,

00:02:36.350 --> 00:02:38.010
and online for the at-home audience

00:02:38.010 --> 00:02:39.290
at the same time.

00:02:39.290 --> 00:02:40.590
Viewers who watch in theaters

00:02:40.590 --> 00:02:42.020
pay the normal price per ticket

00:02:42.020 --> 00:02:44.450
for admission, and
those who watch at home,

00:02:44.450 --> 00:02:45.730
might pay twice,

00:02:45.730 --> 00:02:47.320
once for a monthly subscription

00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:49.100
and depending on the streaming service,

00:02:49.100 --> 00:02:52.070
again with a surcharge for
early access to the film.

00:02:52.070 --> 00:02:54.930
Unlike box office,
retail DVD and licensing,

00:02:54.930 --> 00:02:56.770
streaming surcharges and subscription fees

00:02:56.770 --> 00:02:59.020
are usually not split with the talent.

00:02:59.020 --> 00:03:00.450
Depending on the actor's contract,

00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:02.560
they many still retain
their backend residuals

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:05.010
for things like DVD sales, foreign sales

00:03:05.010 --> 00:03:06.130
and pay per views.

00:03:06.130 --> 00:03:08.410
But those have always been
far less lucrative compared

00:03:08.410 --> 00:03:10.270
to box office bonuses.

00:03:10.270 --> 00:03:11.330
With direct to streaming,

00:03:11.330 --> 00:03:13.160
studios have found a promising solution

00:03:13.160 --> 00:03:14.670
for poor box office sales.

00:03:14.670 --> 00:03:15.790
Actors and agents argue

00:03:15.790 --> 00:03:18.400
that same-day streaming
is in direct competition

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:19.970
with the theatrical release

00:03:19.970 --> 00:03:23.390
and they're taking a huge
loss in backend residuals.

00:03:23.390 --> 00:03:26.640
- Well, Disney says they
made about $60 million

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:28.710
just from the digital side of that

00:03:28.710 --> 00:03:30.130
when they released "Black Widow."

00:03:30.130 --> 00:03:31.680
- [Narrator] Dan Gallagher,
who's been following the rise

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:33.320
in popularity of streaming platforms

00:03:33.320 --> 00:03:34.620
for The Wall Street Journal,

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:35.930
says Disney's stockholders

00:03:35.930 --> 00:03:38.670
really wanted to see them
invest in the streaming future.

00:03:38.670 --> 00:03:40.750
- When you put something
out like "Black Widow"

00:03:40.750 --> 00:03:43.010
where people have a choice on the day of,

00:03:43.010 --> 00:03:44.340
there's always gonna be some number

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:45.440
that choose that convenience.

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:48.230
So it really can't help
but to cannibalize some

00:03:48.230 --> 00:03:49.560
of that theatrical return.

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:51.000
- [Narrator] At least
two million households

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:53.100
purchased the $30 early access stream

00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:54.960
on the film's opening weekend.

00:03:54.960 --> 00:03:56.970
Johansson's lawsuit
argues that her contract

00:03:56.970 --> 00:03:58.730
with Disney's Marvel Entertainment

00:03:58.730 --> 00:04:01.220
guaranteed an exclusive
theatrical release,

00:04:01.220 --> 00:04:03.560
and her salary was tied to
the box office performance

00:04:03.560 --> 00:04:04.393
of the film.

00:04:04.393 --> 00:04:06.340
However, the film was released in theaters

00:04:06.340 --> 00:04:08.660
and on Disney+ at the same time,

00:04:08.660 --> 00:04:10.760
a decision that a person
familiar with the details

00:04:10.760 --> 00:04:14.420
of her contract said would
cost her more than $50 million.

00:04:14.420 --> 00:04:17.050
Disney said it fully compiled
with Johansson's contract

00:04:17.050 --> 00:04:18.650
and that the release of "Black Widow"

00:04:18.650 --> 00:04:21.940
on Disney+ has significantly
enhanced the actor's ability

00:04:21.940 --> 00:04:23.960
to earn additional compensation.

00:04:23.960 --> 00:04:26.120
Digital film releases are
happening more and more.

00:04:26.120 --> 00:04:27.240
According to Reelgood,

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:29.070
straight-to-video
on-demand movie premieres

00:04:29.070 --> 00:04:32.010
have more than tripled
in Q4 year over year.

00:04:32.010 --> 00:04:34.280
In 2020, 228 film titles

00:04:34.280 --> 00:04:36.530
went straight to subscription
videos on demand.

00:04:36.530 --> 00:04:38.010
The pandemic played a large part

00:04:38.010 --> 00:04:40.530
in accelerating the shift
to premiering films online

00:04:40.530 --> 00:04:42.280
but that option was already in motion

00:04:42.280 --> 00:04:44.210
before theaters began to close.

00:04:44.210 --> 00:04:46.760
- I definitely think that this is a path

00:04:46.760 --> 00:04:49.770
that perhaps we were always headed down.

00:04:49.770 --> 00:04:51.600
I just think that it
might have been a couple

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:53.040
of years down the road,

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:55.790
and now because of COVID,
it made it go fast.

00:04:55.790 --> 00:04:57.050
- [Narrator] So what are contracts going

00:04:57.050 --> 00:04:58.830
to look like moving forward?

00:04:58.830 --> 00:05:00.130
Matallana says that actors will need

00:05:00.130 --> 00:05:01.360
to negotiate contracts

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:04.530
that focus far less on
box office ticket sales,

00:05:04.530 --> 00:05:06.950
and A list actors will
likely try to negotiate a cut

00:05:06.950 --> 00:05:08.250
of any surcharge fees

00:05:08.250 --> 00:05:10.250
for early access to their new films.

00:05:10.250 --> 00:05:11.620
Actors with enough star power

00:05:11.620 --> 00:05:13.230
may even be entitled to revenue

00:05:13.230 --> 00:05:15.860
from new subscribers
during a film's release.

00:05:15.860 --> 00:05:18.860
- 2020 was a very ideal environment

00:05:18.860 --> 00:05:20.743
for streaming because people
didn't have a lot of options.

00:05:20.743 --> 00:05:24.180
In a world that people have
more entertainment options,

00:05:24.180 --> 00:05:25.890
it's gonna normalize to something.

00:05:25.890 --> 00:05:29.660
I think bigger than it
was prior to the pandemic.

00:05:29.660 --> 00:05:31.310
- [Narrator] Streaming new movies at home

00:05:31.310 --> 00:05:32.750
is convenient for viewers,

00:05:32.750 --> 00:05:35.110
and for streaming
platforms, very profitable.

00:05:35.110 --> 00:05:36.600
Agents and actors know this,

00:05:36.600 --> 00:05:38.310
and experts say that moving forward,

00:05:38.310 --> 00:05:39.590
they'll start looking for new

00:05:39.590 --> 00:05:41.670
and creative ways to negotiate their way

00:05:41.670 --> 00:05:43.690
into contracts that cut themselves in

00:05:43.690 --> 00:05:45.403
on the streaming profits.

